`,s.jsx(h,{items:[{q:"What does the margin in triplet loss prevent?",a:"Embedding collapse. Without it the loss is satisfied as soon as the negative is further than the positive by any infinitesimal amount, which a degenerate solution mapping every image to nearly the same point achieves trivially. The margin demands a quantitative gap, forcing the representation to actually spread out."},{q:"In what sense is InfoNCE's temperature a mining strategy?",a:"As temperature goes to zero the softmax concentrates almost all gradient on the single hardest negative, which is exactly what hard-negative mining does; as it grows large all negatives are weighted equally and little separation pressure remains. So one scalar controls the same trade-off triplet loss needs an explicit sampling algorithm for. Around 0.07 is the common choice."}]}),`
